## Introduction
With the realization of open structures and permanent porosity in some prototype metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) such as MOF-5 and HKUST-1,1,2 extensive research attention has been paid toward the implementation of functional sites within MOFs for their specific recognition and thus functional properties. In fact, by immobilizing open metal sites as well as Lewis acidic and basic sites into the pore surfaces of MOFs, a series of unique MOFs have been developed for gas storage, separation, catalysis, and sensing.3-21 Because of the very explosive nature of acetylene, the incorporation of specific sites within porous materials is particularly important to enhance their interactions with acetylene molecules and thus for high acetylene storage at room temperature and under a pressure of $0.2~\mathrm{MPa}$ (the compression limit for the safe storage of acetylene).22 Previous efforts on porous materials and MOFs for acetylene storage have been mainly focused on those with small pores to enhance their acetylene adsorption enthalpies which have limited their uptake capacities, with the highest ever reported being 106 cm3/g.23-32
To make use of open metal sites to direct their interactions with acetylene molecules, herein we report HKUST-1 for exceptionally high acetylene uptake up to $201~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $295\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. Neutron powder diffraction studies on acetylene loaded HKUST-1 clearly establish the preferred interactions between the fully deuterated acetylene and open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites with a $\mathrm{Cu-C}$ bonding distance of $2.{\dot{6}}2{\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ . The binding properties of the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2\bar{+}}$ site have been further elucidated by first-principles calculations based on density-functional theory (DFT).
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of HKUST-1. A solution of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2.5}$ (0.124 g $\mathrm{;0.532mmol}$ ) and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ $\mathrm{0.074\g}$ , $0.352\mathrm{mmol}$ ) in the mixture of $N{,}N{\ }$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) (6 mL)/ethanol $(6~\mathrm{mL})/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (6 mL) in a sealed vial $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ gave green, cubic microcrystalline crystals of HKUST-1 ( $\mathrm{120mg}$ ) which were confirmed by a powder X-ray diffraction study. The as-synthesized HKUST-1 was exchanged with acetone 10 times (each of $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) and then activated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under high vacuum up to 4 $\mu\mathrm{mHg}$ before BET and acetylene adsorption studies.
Neutron Powder Diffraction Studies. As-synthesized HKUST-1 with a $\mathrm{DMF/EtOH/H_{2}O}$ mother liquid had been exchanged with excessive methanol 8 times for 2 days. The solvent exchanged sample was then degassed at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $18\mathrm{~h~}$ under high dynamic vacuum. After degassing, the sample was immediately transferred into a vanadium sample can sealed with an indium O-ring, which was later mounted onto a sample stick with a gas loading line connected to an external valve. Both the gas loading line and the sample can were evacuated to high vacuum before the neutron experiments. During all the sample handling after the sample was degassed, the sample was not exposed to air. The sample stick was then put into a top-loading helium closed-cycle refrigerator (CCR). The known amount of fully deuterated acetylene gas $\mathbf{\Gamma}({\bf C}_{2}{\bf D}_{2})$ was first loaded to a calibrated volume and then was exposed to the sample. A temperature reading from a sensor above the sample can was recorded as the sample temperature. All of the gas loading was performed at room temperature before the sample can was cooled down to $4\mathrm{~K~}$ for the measurements.
A total of $0.6492{\mathrm{~g}}$ of HKUST-1 was used for the neutron powder diffraction experiments. The neutron experiments were performed at the high resolution neutron powder diffractometer (BT1) at National Institute of Standards and Technology Center for Neutron Research (NCNR). The sample was measured sequentially at two different gas loadings corresponding to $0.62\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathtt{C u}$ and 1.5 $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu. When loading $0.62{\dot{\mathbf{C}}}_{2}{\mathbf{D}}_{2}$ per $\mathtt{C u}$ , the sample can together with the CCR was originally at room temperature and was slowly cooled down to $4\mathrm{~K~}$ that took ${\sim}6{-}7\mathrm{~h~}$ . For the loading of $1.5\ \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu, the CCR had to remain at $4\mathrm{~K~}$ to shorten the waiting time during the gas loading before starting measurements. The sample stick was taken out of the CCR to load the gas at room temperature. After exposing the sample to the gas at room temperature, the sample stick was then directly put into the CCR again. It took at least $10\mathrm{min}$ before the sample temperature reached $200~\mathrm{K}$ , above which the pressure gauge already read zero. Since the boiling point of bulk acetylene is ${\sim}190\mathrm{K}$ , we expect that there should be no solid acetylene formed in our experiments. The sample was then cooled down to $4\mathrm{K}$ for the measurements. After measuring the sample with $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ gas, the gas was pumped out at room temperature and the bare material was measured again at $4\mathrm{~K~}$ .
First-Principles Calculations. First-principles calculations based on DFT were performed using the PWSCF package.39 We used Vanderbilt-type ultrasoft pseudopotentials and tested both the localdensity approximation (LDA) with the Perdew-Zunger exchange correlation and the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) with the Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof exchange correlation. A cutoff energy of $544\mathrm{eV}$ and a gamma point $k$ sampling were found to be sufficient for the total energy to converge within $0.5\ \mathrm{meV/atom}$ . We first optimized the primitive cell of the MOF structure. To obtain the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule was introduced to various adsorption sites, followed by structural relaxations. As a reference, a $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule placed in a supercell with the same cell dimensions was also relaxed. The static binding energy was then calculated using $E_{\mathrm{B}}=E(\mathrm{MOF})+E(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}})-E(\mathrm{MOF}+\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}})$ .
## Results and Discussions
Variable MOF Structures, Porosities, and Acetylene Adsorption Capacities. For comparison, six prototype microporous MOFs, namely HKUST-1,2 MOF-505,33 MOF-508,34 MIL-53,35 MOF-5,36 and ZIF-8,37 were examined for their acetylene storage. They can be classified into three types of pore structures featuring open metal sites (HKUST-1 and MOF-505), small pores (MOF-508 and MIL-53), and large pores (MOF-5 and ZIF-8), respectively, as shown in Figure 1. HKUST-1 has 3D intersectional pores with a window size of $6.9\textup{\AA}$ and cage size of $10.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (purple sphere). Additionally, there are eight small pockets of $\mathbf{\widehat{5.3\mathrm{~\AA~}}}$ (yellow sphere) around each cage after the terminal water molecules are removed after the activation (Figure 1a). MOF-505 features 1D pores in which a small cage of $\mathbf{\check{6.0\:\AA}}$ (yellow sphere) and large cage of $9.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (purple sphere) are alternately stacked along the $\scriptstyle{c}$ axis (Figure 1b). MOF-508 and MIL-53 are doubly interpenetrated and rod-packing MOFs, exhibiting a 1D straight channel of $4.0\times4.0$ and $7.7\times7.7\mathrm{~\AA^{2}}$ , respectively (Figure 1c, d). MOF-5 (or IRMOF-1) is one of the most highly porous MOFs with very large open 3D pores of $\mathrm{\sim}11.5\mathrm{\AA}$ (Figure 1e). The representative zeolitic MOF, ZIF-8, has a pore aperture of $3.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and cage size of $11.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure 1f). These microporous MOFs were confirmed by powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction and then activated and examined by $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption to establish their permanent porosity. HKUST-1, MOF-505, MOF-508, MIL-53, MOF-5, and ZIF-8 have a Langmuir surface area of 2095, 1694, 946, 1233, 3610, and $1758\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively.
The acetylene uptake were carried out at $295~\mathrm{K}$ (MOF-508 was examined at $290~\mathrm{K}$ to secure its saturated adsorption at 1 atm). As shown in Figure 2, those with large pores (MOF-5 and ZIF-8) are certainly not favorable for acetylene storage, although their surface areas are quite high. The ones with small pores (MOF-508 and MIL-53) take up a moderate amount of acetylene, which are comparable to those reported. ${}^{23-32}\mathrm{MOF}.$ 508 exhibits hysteresis sorption behavior because of the opendense framework transformation34 (Supporting Information, Figures S6 and 7), and its acetylene uptake can be saturated at low pressure. Remarkably, HKUST-1 and MOF-505 with open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites take up a significantly larger amount of acetylene, 201 and $148~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, at $295\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1 atm. The comparison of some microporous MOFs for acetylene storage is listed in Table 1.
Coverage-Dependent Adsorption Enthalpies. The coveragedependent adsorption enthalpies of the MOFs to acetylene were calculated based on a virial method, a well established and reliable methodology from fits of their adsorption isotherms at 273 and $295\mathrm{~K~}$ .38 As shown in Figure 3, HKUST-1 exhibits the largest adsorption enthalpies of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(30.4~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at a coverage of $1\mathrm{mmol/g}\mathrm{,}$ ), while ZIF-8 has the smallest one (13.3 $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at a coverage of $0.02\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ ). As investigated before, the smaller pores favor the stronger interactions between acetylene molecules and pore surfaces. Therefore, MIL-53 has higher adsorption enthalpies than MOF-5.28 MOF-5 has stronger interactions with acetylene than ZIF-8, partly because of the stronger $\mathrm{C-H}^{\bullet\bullet}\pi$ interactions between acetylene and phenyl groups in MOF-5.40 The open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites within HKUST-1 and MOF-505 significantly enhance their interactions with acetylene molecules,41 as clearly demonstrated by the comparison of their adsorption enthalpies with those in MIL-53 whose pore sizes $(7.7\textup{\AA})$ are in the same range with those in HKUST-1 and MOF505 $(5.3-10.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). The smaller pocket of $5.3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (yellow sphere, Figure 1a) and more dense open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites within HKUST-1 might contribute to its stronger interactions with acetylene than MOF-505.
Binding Sites within HKUST-1 for Acetylene Storage. Interestingly, the amount of acetylene gas adsorbed in HKUST-1 at room temperature is $201~\mathrm{cm^{3}/g}$ at 1 atm corresponding to 1.67 $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per Cu. If we assume that each open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site and the entrance window of a small cage can adsorb one acetylene molecule, as previously established in HKUST-1 as initial hydrogen adsorption sites,8 the theoretical maximum amount of stored acetylene gas by these two sites exactly corresponds to $1.67\ C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per Cu. To experimentally determine these adsorption sites, we have measured the activated HKUST-1, $0.62\ \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{cu}$ and $1.5\ C_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{cu}$ loaded HKUST-1, respectively. The data are analyzed with difference Fourier map and Rietveld refinement methods to locate acetylene adsorption sites.7-13
The diffraction pattern of the activated bare material had been measured (see Supporting Information). The excellent agreement of the experimental pattern with the theoretical calculation result $(\chi^{2}=0.{\overset{\cdot}{8}}199)$ indicates that our degassed sample is clean and does not contain any coordinated and free solvent molecules. To identify the first strongest adsorption site, we have first loaded $0.62\mathrm{\C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{cu}$ gas into the sample. Figure 4 is the difference Fourier map of the positive neutron scattering intensity after loading $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ $0.62\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu) calculated by comparing the current diffraction pattern with that of the activated bare material. The pink blobs on top of open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (green balls) show the extra positive intensity attributed from the adsorbed $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ molecules. This conclusiVely proves our previous speculation that the large adsorption enthalpy observed in HKUST-1 is due to the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2\bar{+}}$ sites for their strong binding with acetylene molecules.
To identify the location of $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ more accurately, the diffraction powder pattern is further analyzed with the Rietveld refinement $(\chi^{2}=0.8517)$ . The diffraction pattern together with the calculation results was shown in Figure 5. The refinement results indicate that acetylene is adsorbed exclusively at an open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site at $0.62\mathrm{\C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{cu}$ loading; i.e., all acetylene gas molecules are adsorbed at an open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site, and only one $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ can be adsorbed at each $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site. The $\mathrm{Cu-C}$ distance between the $\mathtt{C u}$ atom and a $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ molecule is $2.62\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is slightly longer than $\operatorname{Ag-C}$ bond distances $(2.354-2.521\mathrm{~\AA~})$ in $[\mathrm{Ag}(\eta_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2})_{3}]^{+}$ and $\mathrm{[Ag(\eta_{2}{-}C_{2}H_{2})_{4}]^{+}}$ molecular compounds.41 The $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ molecule orientates in two possible directions that are parallel to the $_{\mathrm{O-Cu-O}}$ axes, possibly because of the Coulomb attractions between the positively charged D atom of acetylene molecules and negatively charged O atom of carboxylate groups. Each orientation has half occupancy. The $\mathrm{C-}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{C-D}$ bond distance of a $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ molecule is 1.13 and $1.06\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively.
To identify the second strongest binding site of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ , we increased the loading of acetylene into HKUST-1 to $1.5\:\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ gas and then examined the corresponding neutron powder diffraction pattern. As shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the second strongest adsorption site (pink blobs) is located at the entrance window of a small cage (cage window site). The adsorbed acetylene molecules are disordered because of the cubic crystal symmetry of HKUST-1. The acetylene molecules located at the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites and the entrance windows of the small cages contribute to $1.2~\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu, slightly lower than the actually loaded acetylene molecules of $1.5{\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{D}}_{2}$ per Cu. The remaining acetylene molecules of $0.3\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{D}_{2}$ per Cu might be scattered within the large and small cages which have basically negligible contributions to the neutron diffraction pattern (Supporting Information). The neutron powder diffraction studies thus conclusively demonstrate the first and second strongest acetylene binding sites of HKUST-1 to be the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and the cage window site that are the most important binding sites responsible for the exceptionally high acetylene uptake of HKUST-1 at room temperature and 1 atm.
First-Principles Total-Energy Calculations. To understand the ${\bf C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}{-}\mathrm{MOF}$ interactions further, we performed first-principles total-energy calculations on HKUST-1. In agreement with the experimental observation from neutron powder diffraction, our calculations suggest that the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site and cage-window site are indeed the most energetically stable adsorption centers in HKUST-1. The calculated static $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies for these two sites using LDA are 44.8 and $29.2~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively. The corresponding values derived using GGA are 15.8 and $9.3\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ . As expected, LDA overestimates while GGA underestimates the binding strength, so the calculated acetylene binding strength matches quite well with the experimental data. Importantly, the relative calculated binding strengths of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ on the two sites are consistent with the experimental finding that the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ site becomes populated first.
While the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}.$ -framework interaction at the cage-window site is of typical van der Waals type, the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding with the unsaturated $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ is relatively strong. We attribute the high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity on the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ center to the enhanced Coulomb interaction between the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule and the open ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ charge density, similar to what was previously established for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ adsorption on the open metal sites in MOFs.10-12 Figure 6 shows the projected atomic charges of the $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule on the open $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site in HKUST-1 from a Lo¨wdin population analysis.42 Unlike the nonpolar, free $\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ molecule, the adsorbed $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is slightly distorted with an induced dipole moment. As a consequence, there exists an enhanced Coulomb interaction between the distorted $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule and the open metal charge density.
Concluding Remarks. The exceptional high acetylene storage capacity of HKUST-1 at room temperature and atmosphere pressure highlights the very promise of the immobilization of open metal sites to target some useful microporous MOF materials as a practical acetylene storage media. Given the fact that acetylene is a very important raw material for various industrial chemicals, consumer products, and oxy-acetylene cutting in metal fabrication, the realization of such a safe and high-density acetylene storage media will certainly facilitate the safe transportation and wider usage of acetylene in the future.43,44
